### Type
- **Headings break into balanced lines.** When a heading needs two lines, both lines should be about the same length. Never leave a single word alone on a line ("Trigger a load test from your CI / Pipeline" is wrong). Put `text-wrap: balance` on every heading.
  - Section headings are usually two lines: a grey lead line and a black main line, each 2 to 6 words.
  - A short heading stays on one line.
- Manrope for all text. Space Mono for code, IDs and small uppercase labels. Geist Pixel for big numbers only.
- Weights are 400 for body and 500 for headings, buttons and emphasis. Never 700.

| Style | Size/line | Tracking | Weight |
|---|---|---|---|
| H1 | 56/64 | -2px | 500 |
| H2 | 48/58 | -2px | 500 |
| H3 | 32/40 | -1px | 500 |
| H4 | 24/32 | -1px | 500 |
| H5 | 18/26 | -0.5px | 500 |
| H6 | 14/22 | 0px | 500 |
| H7 | 12/18 | 0px | 500 |
| B1 | 18/28 | 0px | 400 |
| B2 | 16/24 | 0px | 400 |
| B3 | 14/22 | 0px | 400 |
| B4 | 12/18 | 0px | 400 |
| B5 | 10/14 | 0px | 400 |

### Page structure
- **A block is heading, then description, then content** (cards, boxes, code, media), and nothing after the content.
  - No closing caption, footnote, fine print or muted sentence under the cards.
  - If something must be said, put it in the description above, or make it a button or a text link.

### Space, size and shape
- **Spacing:** use only 0, 2, 4, 6, 8, 12, 16, 20, 24, 28, 32, 40, 44, 48, 56, 64, 72, 80, 96, 112, 128 px.
- **Corners:** every surface is sharp (0): images, cards, boxes, panels, tables, sections, modals.
  - Controls are 6px: buttons, inputs, selects, segment controls.
  - Small labels and tags are 4px. Pills, avatars and toggles are fully round.
  - No other radius exists. Never round a card or an image.
- **Buttons:** 48 / 44 / 40 px tall.
- **Fields:** 44 / 40 / 38 px tall.
- **Icons:** Lucide only, at 16 or 20 px, `stroke-width="1.5"` on the 24 viewBox, in Fg/default.
- **Focus:** a 2px `#1742DF` ring.
- **Layout:** auto layout only (flex, padding and gap from the spacing scale). There is no column grid. Content sits in a fixed 1232px frame on every screen from 1024px up (1440, 1920 and 2560 alike); only the side margins grow:

| Screen | Designed at | From | Outer margin | Frame | Inner padding | Section spacing |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| Mobile | 390 | 0 | 16px | full width | 16px | 64px |
| Tablet | 768 | 640 | 32px | full width | 32px | 96px |
| Desktop | 1440 | 1024 | 104px (auto) | 1232px | 40px | 128px |
| Wide | 1920 | 1680 | 344px (auto) | 1232px | 40px | 128px |
| Ultra | 2560 | 2240 | 664px (auto) | 1232px | 40px | 128px |

  Use `var(--tm-frame)`, `var(--tm-frame-padding)`, `var(--tm-margin)` and `var(--tm-section-y)`. They switch at each size on their own.
- **Card frames by default.** Boxes sit in joined card frames. The user will not ask for this; do it on your own whenever a request has two or more boxes side by side.
  - That covers cards, tiles, features, stats, plans, steps, logos, team members, links and any other "N boxes".
  - Wrap them in `<div class="tm-join">`. Each direct child is one box.
  - The row fits itself to the frame, never more than 4 per row: 2, 3 or 4 boxes sit in one row (add `tm-join--square` to show 4 as 2 × 2), 5 make 3 + 2, 6 make 3 × 2, 7 make 4 + 3, 8 make 4 × 2, 9 make 3 × 3, 10 make 4 + 3 + 3. Shorter rows stretch so no slot is left empty. Tablets show 2 per row and phones 1.
  - The boxes touch with 1px `#DDDCD6` hairlines, run edge to edge to the frame lines, are sharp (0 radius) and white, with padding from `var(--tm-frame-padding)`.
  - Never add a gap, margin, shadow or radius between or on these boxes. Never use a fixed number of columns that leaves a hole.
  - Without the kit CSS, rebuild the same thing: `display: grid; gap: 1px; background: #DDDCD6;` with at most 4 columns, shorter last rows stretched to fill, and white cells.
  - Use separate floating cards only when the user asks for them by name.
- **Shadows:** only the `--tm-shadow-*` tokens.

### Guide the person, like a designer would
Most people asking you to build will not know design. Do not just build the literal words; lead them.
1. **Get the goal in one line:** what the page or screen is for, who it is for, and the one action it should drive. Ask only if you truly cannot tell.
2. **Propose before you build anything bigger than one block.** List the outline with one line each on why, for example: hero, logo wall, feature pointers, big cards with boxes, reviews, pricing, CTA, footer.
3. **Offer two or three options when a choice changes the look,** and name the one you recommend. For example: show two products as big painted cards with a box row, or as three image cards. If they don't pick, go with your recommendation.
4. **Fill the gaps with TestMu defaults:**
   - card frames;
   - balanced two-line headings;
   - sentence-case copy and realistic sample content;
   - painted nature images behind product UI;
   - one primary action per block.
5. **Match common asks to the kit:**

| The person asks for | Use |
|---|---|
| features or capabilities | feature pointers or bento |
| two main products | big cards, alone or with boxes |
| how it works or steps | steps |
| prices or plans | pricing |
| testimonials | reviews |
| customer logos | logo wall |
| numbers or proof | more reasons |
| questions | FAQ |
| a sign-up push | CTA, or the exit popup |
| a list of guides or use cases | catalog |
| docs-style browsing | catalog with left nav |

6. **When it's built,** say in two or three plain sentences what you chose and what they can change next. Offer the next useful step, for example adding pricing or a customer story.

### Building new things
- Build what the user asks for from these rules. New layouts and new components are expected, and they must still use only these tokens.
- **Controls:** for buttons, fields, checkboxes, tabs and similar, use the TestMu classes in `testmu.css`: `tm-btn`, `tm-ibtn`, `tm-tbtn`, `tm-field`, `tm-check`, `tm-radio`, `tm-lbl`, `tm-navbar`, `tm-seg`, `tm-disc`, `tm-pager`, `tm-select`, `tm-token`, `tm-tooltip`, `tm-navlist`, `tm-crumbs`.
- **Page sections:** borrow from the section kit (`tm-fold` and the rest) when one fits.
- **New CSS:** compose it only from `var(--tm-*)` tokens.
- **Copy:** sentence case, short, no exclamation marks. Write "TestMu AI", never "LambdaTest".

### Check before you finish
Run `node .testmu/bin/ds-check.mjs --fix <changed files or folder>`. It snaps any off-system color, font, font size, weight or spacing to the nearest TestMu token and prints what it changed. Corners follow the element: buttons, inputs and selects get 6px, tags, labels and badges 4px, pills, chips, avatars and toggles round, and everything else 0.

It also runs on its own:
- after every edit and every shell command in Claude Code, after every edit in Cursor, and at the end of each turn in both;
- on every git commit, whichever agent or person wrote the code;
- on every pull request (GitHub Actions), so nothing off the system merges.

Never get around it: do not write style files through shell commands to skip the check, do not commit with `--no-verify`, and do not add `ds-ignore` unless the user asks for that exact value.
